ML_PAGE("l2-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l2\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 2 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Lecture 2 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 68 multiple-choice questions and 8 coding questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them. Every question also has a <b>harder version</b> on the same concept, one reveal below its answer.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>68</strong><span>MCQs in the notes, each with a harder version</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>8</strong><span>Coding questions</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Bloom challenge questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l2\" data-quiz=\"2\">Start Lecture 2 simulator</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"2\" data-quiz-mode=\"hard\">Hard mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"2\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Bloom challenge</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l2\" id=\"l2p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">42 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Lecture 2 - MCQ Exam Preparation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s0\" title=\"Lecture 2 - MCQ Exam Preparation\">Overview</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - THE PIPELINE &amp; CRISP-DM</li><li data-full=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s2\" title=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Key facts table</button></li><li data-full=\"2. The biggest MCQ trap: CRISP-DM vs ML Pipeline\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s3\" title=\"2. The biggest MCQ trap: CRISP-DM vs ML Pipeline\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Trap: CRISP-DM vs pipeline</button></li><li data-full=\"3. CRISP-DM order\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s4\" title=\"3. CRISP-DM order\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>CRISP-DM phase order</button></li><li data-full=\"4. MCQ Practice - Set 1\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s5\" title=\"4. MCQ Practice - Set 1\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Practice: CRISP-DM phases</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - DATA SOURCES &amp; TYPES</li><li data-full=\"5. Data-format MCQs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s7\" title=\"5. Data-format MCQs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Practice: data formats</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Web scraping trap\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s8\" title=\"6. Web scraping trap\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Web scraping</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Data types\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s9\" title=\"7. Data types\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Data types</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C - INSPECTING &amp; CLEANING</li><li data-full=\"8. `df.info()` vs `df.describe()`\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s11\" title=\"8. df.info() vs df.describe()\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>info() vs describe()</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Missing-value questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s12\" title=\"9. Missing-value questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Practice: missing values</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Missing-data scenario trap\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s13\" title=\"10. Missing-data scenario trap\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Missing-data scenario</button></li><li data-full=\"11. Duplicate questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s14\" title=\"11. Duplicate questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Practice: duplicates</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Duplicate leakage trap\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s15\" title=\"12. Duplicate leakage trap\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Duplicates and leakage</button></li><li data-full=\"13. Type conversion\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s16\" title=\"13. Type conversion\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Type conversion</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Filtering and selection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s17\" title=\"14. Filtering and selection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Filtering and selection</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D - FEATURE ENGINEERING, ENCODING &amp; SCALING</li><li data-full=\"15. Feature engineering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s19\" title=\"15. Feature engineering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Feature engineering</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Categorical encoding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s20\" title=\"16. Categorical encoding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Categorical encoding</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Encoding MCQs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s21\" title=\"17. Encoding MCQs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Practice: encoding</button></li><li data-full=\"18. Feature scaling\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s22\" title=\"18. Feature scaling\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Feature scaling</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Scaling MCQs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s23\" title=\"19. Scaling MCQs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Practice: scaling</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Simple scaling calculation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s24\" title=\"20. Simple scaling calculation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Min-max calculation</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART E - TEXT, SUMMARISATION &amp; VISUALISATION</li><li data-full=\"21. Basic text preprocessing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s26\" title=\"21. Basic text preprocessing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Text preprocessing</button></li><li data-full=\"22. `groupby()` - extremely likely practical MCQ\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s27\" title=\"22. groupby() - extremely likely practical MCQ\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>groupby</button></li><li data-full=\"23. `groupby()` questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s28\" title=\"23. groupby() questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Practice: groupby</button></li><li data-full=\"24. Visualization\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s29\" title=\"24. Visualization\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Choosing a chart</button></li><li data-full=\"25. Visualization MCQs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s30\" title=\"25. Visualization MCQs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>Practice: charts</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART F - DATA LEAKAGE</li><li data-full=\"26. The most important trap: data leakage\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s32\" title=\"26. The most important trap: data leakage\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Data leakage</button></li><li data-full=\"27. Leakage MCQs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s33\" title=\"27. Leakage MCQs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Practice: leakage</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART G - CODING QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"28. Coding Question 1 - inspection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s35\" title=\"28. Coding Question 1 - inspection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Coding: inspection</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Coding Question 2 - missing values\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s36\" title=\"29. Coding Question 2 - missing values\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Coding: missing values</button></li><li data-full=\"30. Coding Question 3 - duplicates\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s37\" title=\"30. Coding Question 3 - duplicates\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Coding: duplicates</button></li><li data-full=\"31. Coding Question 4 - type conversion\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s38\" title=\"31. Coding Question 4 - type conversion\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Coding: type conversion</button></li><li data-full=\"32. Coding Question 5 - categorical encoding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s39\" title=\"32. Coding Question 5 - categorical encoding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Coding: encoding</button></li><li data-full=\"33. Coding Question 6 - filtering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s40\" title=\"33. Coding Question 6 - filtering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Coding: filtering</button></li><li data-full=\"34. Coding Question 7 - engineered datetime feature\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s41\" title=\"34. Coding Question 7 - engineered datetime feature\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Coding: date feature</button></li><li data-full=\"35. Coding Question 8 - groupby\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s42\" title=\"35. Coding Question 8 - groupby\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Coding: groupby</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART H - HARDER SCENARIOS &amp; ANSWER KEYS</li><li data-full=\"36. Harder scenario questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s44\" title=\"36. Harder scenario questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Harder scenarios</button></li><li data-full=\"37. Answer key for Q1-Q19\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s45\" title=\"37. Answer key for Q1-Q19\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Answer key: Q1-19</button></li><li data-full=\"38. Answer key for Q20-Q44\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s46\" title=\"38. Answer key for Q20-Q44\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Answer key: Q20-44</button></li><li data-full=\"39. Answer key for Q45-Q68\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s47\" title=\"39. Answer key for Q45-Q68\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>Answer key: Q45-68</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART I - FINAL REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"40. The code you should be able to recognise immediately\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s49\" title=\"40. The code you should be able to recognise immediately\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Key pandas code</button></li><li data-full=\"41. The exam traps I&#x27;d prioritize\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2p-s50\" title=\"41. The exam traps I&#x27;d prioritize\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>Top traps</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l2\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 2 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Know-cold tables, traps, practice questions and coding questions - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>42 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 2 - MCQ Exam Preparation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Machine Learning Pipeline</h4>\n<p>Lecture 2 is much more practical than Lecture 1. The main things I would expect an MCQ exam to test are:</p>\n<p><strong>CRISP-DM vs ML Pipeline → data formats → pandas inspection → cleaning → missing values → duplicates → type conversion → feature engineering → categorical encoding → scaling → text basics → <code>groupby()</code> → visualization → data leakage.</strong></p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly frames the pipeline as a technical workflow from data retrieval and preparation through modelling, evaluation and deployment.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d1\">PART A - THE PIPELINE &amp; CRISP-DM</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. What you should know instantly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Instinctive answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>CRISP-DM</td>\n<td>Overall project framework</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ML Pipeline</td>\n<td>Technical workflow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business Understanding</td>\n<td>Define problem, objectives, success criteria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data Understanding</td>\n<td>Inspect available data and quality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data Preparation</td>\n<td>Clean, transform, encode, scale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Modeling</td>\n<td>Train/select/tune algorithms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation</td>\n<td>Check whether model meets original objective</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deployment</td>\n<td>Put model into real system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CSV</td>\n<td>Tabular rows/columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JSON</td>\n<td>Key-value / hierarchical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>XML</td>\n<td>Tag-based hierarchy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL</td>\n<td>Relational database tables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>df.info()</code></td>\n<td>Structure, types, non-null counts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>df.describe()</code></td>\n<td>Numeric statistics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dropna()</code></td>\n<td>Remove missing observations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fillna()</code></td>\n<td>Replace missing value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ffill()</code></td>\n<td>Use previous value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bfill()</code></td>\n<td>Use next value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>duplicated()</code></td>\n<td>Identify duplicates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>drop_duplicates()</code></td>\n<td>Remove duplicates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pd.to_datetime()</code></td>\n<td>Convert to datetime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pd.to_numeric()</code></td>\n<td>Convert to numeric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One-hot encoding</td>\n<td>Nominal categories → binary columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scaling</td>\n<td>Put numeric features on comparable scale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min-Max</td>\n<td>Usually 0-1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standardization</td>\n<td>Mean 0, SD 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>groupby()</code></td>\n<td>Split → Apply → Combine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Line</td>\n<td>Trend over time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bar</td>\n<td>Compare categories</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Histogram</td>\n<td>Distribution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Box plot</td>\n<td>Spread/outliers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scatter</td>\n<td>Relationship between two numeric variables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data leakage</td>\n<td>Test-set information influences training/preprocessing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. The biggest MCQ trap: CRISP-DM vs ML Pipeline</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l2_crisp_vs_pipeline\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Not two competing processes: the ML pipeline runs inside CRISP-DM&#x27;s preparation, modelling and evaluation phases.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture says:</p>\n<p><strong>CRISP-DM = project framework</strong></p>\n<p><strong>ML Pipeline = technical workflow</strong></p>\n<p>CRISP-DM includes business strategy and objectives, whereas the ML pipeline is more directly about code, transformations and algorithms.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{CRISP}\\text{-}\\text{DM}\n=\n\\text{Why and what are we solving?}$$</div>\n<p>while:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{ML Pipeline}\n=\n\\text{How technically do we build it?}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. CRISP-DM order</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Know this cold:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Business Understanding → Data Understanding → Data Preparation → Modeling → Evaluation → Deployment\"><span>Business Understanding</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data Understanding</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data Preparation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Modeling</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Deployment</span></div>\n<h5>Memory trick</h5>\n<p><strong>B D D M E D</strong></p>\n<p>Business<br />\nData<br />\nData<br />\nModel<br />\nEvaluate<br />\nDeploy</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. MCQ Practice - Set 1</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>A company says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We want to reduce unexpected equipment downtime.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Which CRISP-DM phase handles this first?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Modeling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Business Understanding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Data Preparation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Deployment</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H1</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A manager says: \"Customers are leaving.\" Which output is the best result of CRISP-DM <strong>Business Understanding</strong>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A trained churn classifier</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A cleaned customer table with no missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A measurable objective, such as \"reduce churn among premium subscribers by 10% this quarter\", with an agreed way to judge success</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A deployed churn dashboard</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Business Understanding turns a vague complaint into a <strong>clear, measurable goal</strong> before any data work starts. A belongs to Modeling, B to Data Preparation and D to Deployment. Starting with them risks solving the wrong problem well.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>The business goal is converted into:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Predict equipment failure within the next 24 hours.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is primarily part of:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Business Understanding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Data Cleaning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Feature Scaling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Deployment</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H2</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>The business goal \"reduce unexpected equipment downtime\" has to be translated into an ML problem. Which of these is <strong>not</strong> a usable ML translation?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> \"Predict whether each machine will fail within the next 24 hours from its recent sensor readings.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> \"Make the machines more reliable.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> \"Estimate each machine's remaining hours before failure.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> \"Flag machines whose sensor patterns look unusual compared with normal operation.\"</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A, C and D each specify a <strong>target or pattern the model can learn from data</strong>: classification, regression or anomaly detection. B just restates the business wish. It names no prediction, so no model can be trained for it. Business Understanding must produce a framing like A, C or D.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>A data scientist investigates available sensor logs, missing values and whether the data is relevant.</p>\n<p>Which phase?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Modeling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Data Understanding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Evaluation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H3</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which activity belongs to <strong>Data Understanding</strong> rather than Data Preparation?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Discovering that 30% of vibration readings are missing, and that sensor C only started logging in March</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Filling the missing vibration readings with the median</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scaling temperature and pressure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> One-hot encoding machine_type</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Data Understanding <strong>investigates</strong> the data: what exists, its quality, its gaps and its relevance. B, C and D <strong>change</strong> the data, which is Data Preparation. You need A's findings before you can decide how to do B.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Encoding categories, handling missing values and scaling numeric features belongs mainly to:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Data Preparation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Business Understanding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Evaluation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H4</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which activity belongs to <strong>Data Preparation</strong>, rather than Data Understanding or Modeling?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Running <code>df.info()</code> to see the column types</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Choosing between KNN and a linear model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Defining what counts as a successful prediction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Converting \"ERROR\" readings to missing values and then imputing them</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Data Preparation <strong>transforms</strong> the raw data into model-ready features: cleaning, imputing, encoding and scaling. A only inspects the data (Data Understanding). B is Modeling. C is Business Understanding.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>Which phase asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Does the model satisfy the business criteria defined earlier?\"</p>\n</blockquote></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Modeling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Evaluation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Data Understanding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Data Retrieval</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H5</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A failure model reaches 95% accuracy. The business requirement agreed earlier was to \"catch at least 80% of real failures\", and the model catches only 40% of them. What should CRISP-DM <strong>Evaluation</strong> conclude?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Deploy it, because 95% accuracy is excellent.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The model doesn't meet the business criterion, so return to earlier phases (data, features, modelling) instead of deploying.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Skip ahead to Business Understanding of a new project.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Evaluation only checks for code errors, so this isn't its concern.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Evaluation compares the model against the <strong>business criteria set at the start</strong>, not against a generic metric. A model with high accuracy that misses most failures fails that test. A is the trap. D misdescribes the phase. C abandons the project instead of iterating on it.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> CRISP-DM and ML Pipeline are exactly the same</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> CRISP-DM is mainly code</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ML Pipeline is more technically focused</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> CRISP-DM begins with feature scaling</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H6</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which item belongs to CRISP-DM but lies <strong>outside</strong> the narrower technical ML pipeline?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Data Preparation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Modeling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Business Understanding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Evaluation &amp; Tuning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The ML pipeline (Data Retrieval → Data Preparation → Modeling → Evaluation &amp; Tuning → Deployment &amp; Monitoring) is the <strong>technical</strong> flow. CRISP-DM wraps it in a business process that starts by understanding the business problem. A, B and D appear in both.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>Which sequence is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Modeling → Business Understanding → Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Business Understanding → Data Understanding → Data Preparation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Deployment → Modeling → Evaluation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Data Preparation → Business Understanding → Modeling</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H7</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which sequence matches the lecture's standard ML pipeline?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Data Preparation → Data Retrieval → Modeling → Deployment &amp; Monitoring → Evaluation &amp; Tuning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Data Retrieval → Data Preparation → Modeling → Evaluation &amp; Tuning → Deployment &amp; Monitoring</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Modeling → Data Retrieval → Data Preparation → Evaluation &amp; Tuning → Deployment &amp; Monitoring</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Data Retrieval → Modeling → Data Preparation → Evaluation &amp; Tuning → Deployment &amp; Monitoring</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>You must <strong>get</strong> the data, <strong>prepare</strong> it, <strong>train</strong> the model, <strong>evaluate and tune</strong> it, and only then <strong>deploy and monitor</strong> it. The model comes late in the pipeline. A prepares data before retrieving it and deploys before evaluating. C and D model before the data exists or is ready.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Which is typically one of the heaviest stages according to the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Data Preparation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Business Understanding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Evaluation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H8</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Why is Data Preparation often one of the heaviest stages?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Real data arrives messy (inconsistent date formats, NaN, \"ERROR\" strings, text categories), and all of it must be fixed and turned into consistent numerical features in a way that can be repeated.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is the only stage that needs a GPU.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Models can't be trained after Data Preparation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Deployment is always automatic, so all remaining effort shifts to preparation.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture's sensor example shows how much must happen before a model can use the data, and the same steps must be applied consistently later. B, C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d6\">PART B - DATA SOURCES &amp; TYPES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Data-format MCQs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture compares four major formats.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>Which format is naturally tabular and generally convenient for pandas?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> JSON</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> CSV</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> XML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> HTML</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H9</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which source can be loaded into a pandas DataFrame <strong>without restructuring</strong>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> An API response in which each customer holds a list of orders</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An XML export with customers nested inside regions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An HTML product page</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A sales export with one row per transaction and fixed columns (date, product, price, quantity)</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>CSV-style data is already <strong>rows and columns</strong>, which is ML's favourite shape. A (JSON) and B (XML) are hierarchical and need flattening. C needs scraping before it becomes a table.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>Which format commonly contains nested key-value pairs?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> CSV</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SQL</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> JSON</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> TXT</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H10</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>An API returns:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">{&quot;user&quot;: {&quot;id&quot;: 7, &quot;orders&quot;: [{&quot;amt&quot;: 50}, {&quot;amt&quot;: 20}]}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which format is this, and what is its main challenge for classical ML?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> XML; its tags must be removed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> CSV; its delimiter is wrong</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> JSON; it is nested (one user holds a list of orders), so it must be flattened and you must decide what one row represents</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> SQL; it needs a join</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The braces and <strong>nested key-value pairs</strong> mark it as JSON. Classical models expect flat rows, so you have to choose one row per user (perhaps aggregating the orders) or one row per order. A, B and D misidentify the format.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>Which format is tag-based and commonly associated with legacy enterprise systems?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> XML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> CSV</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> JSON</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> SQL</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H11</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>&lt;customer&gt;&lt;name&gt;Ali&lt;/name&gt;&lt;plan&gt;Gold&lt;/plan&gt;&lt;/customer&gt;</code> comes from a 20-year-old billing system. Which format is it, and which statement fits the lecture?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> HTML, which is mainly used for API responses</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> XML: a tag-based hierarchy, commonly found in legacy enterprise systems</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> JSON, because it contains names</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> CSV, because it holds one customer</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Opening and closing <strong>tags</strong> in a hierarchy mark XML, and the lecture links XML to <strong>legacy enterprise systems</strong>. A: HTML uses tags too, but for web pages, and APIs typically return JSON. C and D misread the structure.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>Which is most closely associated with relational tables and enterprise databases?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> HTML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SQL</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> JSON</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> CSV</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H12</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Customer details are stored in one table and transactions in another, linked by <code>customer_id</code>. Which data source does this describe, and what is usually needed before modelling?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> JSON; flattening</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Image data; flattening</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> SQL relational tables; combining them via the shared key so that each row has the features you need</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A CSV file; tokenization</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Separate tables linked by a key are the <strong>relational</strong> model behind SQL databases, which hold much real business data. To build one modelling table, you combine the tables on <code>customer_id</code>. A, B and D misidentify the source.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>Why may JSON require additional preprocessing before classical ML?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is always encrypted</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is hierarchical and may need flattening</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It cannot contain numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It contains only images</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H13</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A JSON API returns each customer with a varying number of orders. What is the <strong>real</strong> decision you face when flattening it for classical ML?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Whether to encrypt the file</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> What one row represents (a customer with aggregated order features, or one order per row), because a nested list doesn't fit a single flat row as it is</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Whether JSON can contain numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Which image size to use</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Flattening isn't only a mechanical step: a hierarchy with <strong>variable-length lists</strong> forces a choice of row granularity, and that choice changes what the model learns. A, C and D are irrelevant.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Web scraping trap</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Web scraping broadly involves:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Crawl} \\rightarrow \\text{Scrape}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Crawl:</strong> obtain webpages.</p>\n<p><strong>Scrape:</strong> extract desired information.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>Which step retrieves webpages from URLs?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scraping</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Crawling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scaling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Encoding</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H14</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A bot starts at a product listing page, follows every \"next page\" link and downloads 500 HTML pages. Nothing has been extracted from them yet. Which step is this?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scraping</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Crawling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Data Preparation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Modeling</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Crawling</strong> finds and retrieves pages by following URLs. <strong>Scraping</strong> pulls specific fields out of those pages, which hasn't happened yet. C and D come later.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>Which step extracts specific information from retrieved HTML?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scraping</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Crawling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Modeling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Binning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H15</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>After crawling, the bot reads each downloaded page and saves the product name and price into a table. Which statement is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> This is crawling, because pages are involved.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Scraping must happen before crawling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> This is modelling, because a table is created.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> This is scraping: it extracts targeted information from retrieved HTML and turns unstructured pages into structured rows.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Crawl → Scrape. <strong>Scraping</strong> extracts the desired data from pages that have already been retrieved. A mixes up the two steps. B reverses the order. C confuses data collection with modelling.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Data types</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture distinguishes numeric, categorical, text and datetime data and notes that each requires different preprocessing.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>Which feature is categorical?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Age = 21</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Price = 99.90</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Colour = Red</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Height = 181</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H16</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which column should be treated as <strong>categorical</strong> even though it is stored as a number?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Price = 99.90</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Height = 181</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Age = 21</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Branch code = 101, 205, 330</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Branch codes are <strong>labels</strong>: branch 330 isn't \"more\" than branch 101. Arithmetic on them is meaningless, so they need categorical treatment (encoding). A, B and C are genuine measurements.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>Which is the most likely preprocessing for:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Colour = Red / Blue / Green\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scaling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Categorical encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Tokenization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Date extraction</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H17</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A dataset has Size (Small / Medium / Large) and Colour (Red / Blue / Green). Which encoding plan fits the lecture?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot encode both, because order never matters.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Map both to integers 1-3.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> One-hot encode Size and map Colour to integers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Map Size to ordered integers (Small = 0, Medium = 1, Large = 2) and one-hot encode Colour.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Numerical coding makes sense when categories are <strong>genuinely ordered</strong> (Size). Nominal categories (Colour) need one-hot encoding to avoid a false order. B gives Colour a fake order. C reverses the logic. A works but throws away Size's useful ordering.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>Which is most appropriate for a datetime feature?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Extract month/day/hour</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Always one-hot encode every individual timestamp</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Remove it automatically</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Apply TF-IDF</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H18</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A <code>signup_time</code> column with 50,000 unique timestamps is one-hot encoded. What goes wrong?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing; one-hot encoding suits every column.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The timestamps are deleted.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> You get about 50,000 columns, most used by a single row. No pattern can carry over to new times, so you should extract parts such as month, day of week or hour instead.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The model will treat the timestamps as text.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Each unique timestamp becomes its own <strong>one-off category</strong>, so nothing generalises. Extracted parts (month, weekday, hour) <strong>repeat</strong> across rows and expose patterns. A ignores this. B and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Which preprocessing is most closely associated with natural-language text?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Tokenization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Min-Max scaling only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> SQL join</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Forward fill only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H19</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which order of basic text preprocessing is most sensible?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Remove stopwords → tokenize → lowercase → stem/lemmatize</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Stem/lemmatize → tokenize → remove stopwords → lowercase</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Lowercase → stem/lemmatize → remove stopwords → tokenize</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Tokenize → lowercase → remove stopwords → stem/lemmatize</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>You can't remove or stem <strong>words</strong> until the text is split into tokens. Lowercasing before stopword removal means \"The\" matches \"the\". A, B and C each run a word-level step before tokenization.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d10\">PART C - INSPECTING &amp; CLEANING</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. <code>df.info()</code> vs <code>df.describe()</code></span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This distinction is very likely MCQ material.</p>\n<p>The lecture states that <code>df.info()</code> shows data types and non-null counts, while <code>df.describe()</code> provides summary statistics such as mean, standard deviation, minimum and maximum.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>Which function is best for seeing column data types?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>df.describe()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>df.info()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>df.groupby()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>df.dropna()</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H20</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>df.info()</code> reports the <code>price</code> column as <strong>object</strong> with 1,000 non-null values. What is the most likely situation, and the right action?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The prices are fine; <code>info()</code> always shows object.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The prices are stored as text (for example \"$150.00\"), so strip the symbols and convert with <code>pd.to_numeric()</code>.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> All prices are missing; drop the column.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The prices are dates; use <code>pd.to_datetime()</code>.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><code>info()</code> shows <strong>data types</strong>. A numeric column that shows up as object usually contains text. The value and the data type are different things. A is false. C contradicts the 1,000 non-null values. D has no evidence behind it.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Which function gives mean, standard deviation, min and max?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>df.info()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>df.describe()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>df.ffill()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>df.rename()</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H21</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>df.describe()</code> shows Age with min -3 and max 220. What does this tell you?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Age is perfectly clean.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Age is categorical.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> There are impossible values (data errors), which must be investigated before modelling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>describe()</code> is miscalculating.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><code>describe()</code> gives summary statistics such as min and max, which quickly reveal <strong>impossible values</strong>. Neither a negative age nor an age of 220 is valid. That is a data-quality signal, not a reason to trust the data (A) or to blame the tool (D).</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Suppose a column contains 1000 rows but <code>df.info()</code> reports 940 non-null values.</p>\n<p>How many values are missing?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 40</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 60</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 940</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1000</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H22</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A DataFrame has 1,000 rows. <code>df.info()</code> reports <code>price</code> with 940 non-null values, <code>date</code> with 985 and <code>quantity</code> with 1,000. After <code>df.dropna()</code>, how many rows can remain?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Between 925 and 940</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Exactly 940</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Exactly 925</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Exactly 1,000</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><code>price</code> is missing in 60 rows and <code>date</code> in 15. If all 15 date gaps fall in rows already missing a price, 940 rows survive. If none overlap, $1000 - 60 - 15 = 925$ survive. Without knowing the overlap, the answer is a <strong>range</strong>. B and C assume one particular overlap. D ignores the missing values.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Missing-value questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture presents three broad strategies:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Remove}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Impute}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Forward/Backward fill}$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>What does this do?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.dropna()\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Replaces missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Removes observations containing missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Finds duplicate records</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Converts values to numeric</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H23</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>When is <code>df.dropna()</code> most <strong>risky</strong>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> When only 2 of 100,000 rows have a missing value, spread at random</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> When there are no missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> When missing values are widespread or concentrated in one group (for example, income is missing for most rural customers), so dropping removes a lot of data and biases what remains</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> When the column is numeric</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Removing rows is cheap when the missing values are few and random (A). But if they're <strong>widespread or systematic</strong>, dropping them shrinks the data and can delete whole groups, making the training data unrepresentative. B and D don't make dropna risky.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>What does this do?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;price&quot;].fillna(df[&quot;price&quot;].mean())\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Removes price</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Replaces missing prices with mean price</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Calculates median</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Removes duplicates</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H24</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>price = [100, 200, NaN, 300, NaN]</code>. What does <code>df[\"price\"].fillna(df[\"price\"].mean())</code> produce, and what is a side effect?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> [100, 200, 200, 300, 200]; the column's spread shrinks, because the same value fills several rows</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> [100, 200, 150, 300, 150]; nothing changes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> [100, 200, 0, 300, 0]; the column's spread grows</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The rows with NaN are removed</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The mean of the <strong>known</strong> values is $(100 + 200 + 300)/3 = 200$, so 200 fills both gaps. Putting the same central value in several rows pulls the data towards the middle, which <strong>reduces the variance</strong>. B uses the wrong mean. C fills with zeros. D describes <code>dropna()</code>.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>When is median often preferable to mean for numerical imputation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Strong outliers exist</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> No missing values exist</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The feature is text</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The data contains only categories</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H25</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Salaries are 3,000, 3,200, 3,100, 2,900, 50,000 (the CEO) and NaN. What do mean imputation and median imputation fill in?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Mean 3,100; median 12,440</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mean 12,440; median 3,100: the median is far more representative here</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both give about 3,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Mean 50,000; median 2,900</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Mean $= (3000 + 3200 + 3100 + 2900 + 50000)/5 = 12{,}440$, which the one extreme value pulls upward. Median $= 3{,}100$, the middle of the sorted known values. With strong outliers, the median is the safer choice. A swaps the two values.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>For the values:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">100\n110\n120\n1000\nNaN\n</code></pre>\n<p>which central tendency is less affected by <code>1000</code>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Mean</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Median</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Variance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Range</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Because the extreme value pulls the mean upward.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H26</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>For 100, 110, 120 and 1,000, the mean is 332.5 and the median is 115. If 1,000 is changed to 10,000, what happens?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both stay the same.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both rise sharply.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The mean rises to 2,582.5 while the median stays at 115.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The median rises to 5,000.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The mean uses <strong>every value's size</strong>: $(100 + 110 + 120 + 10000)/4 = 2{,}582.5$. The median depends only on the <strong>middle position</strong>, which still lies between 110 and 120. This is why the median resists outliers.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>Which missing-value strategy is particularly reasonable for ordered/time-series data when the previous state tends to persist?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Forward fill</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> TF-IDF</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Standardization</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H27</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which column is the <strong>worst</strong> candidate for forward fill?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Room temperature logged every minute</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A machine's ON/OFF status, which changes rarely</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Customer income, in a table sorted by customer ID where each row is a different, unrelated customer</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Warehouse stock level between hourly counts</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Forward fill assumes the <strong>previous row's state carries on</strong> into the next, which makes sense in ordered time series (A, B, D). Neighbouring rows of unrelated customers share nothing, so copying one customer's income into the next is invented data.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>What does:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.ffill()\n</code></pre>\n<p>do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Copies the next known value backward</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Copies the previous known value forward</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Deletes all missing rows</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Converts values to float</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H28</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>s = [5, NaN, NaN, 8, NaN]</code>. What does <code>s.ffill()</code> return?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> [5, 8, 8, 8, NaN]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> [5, 5, 5, 8, NaN]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> [5, 6.5, 6.5, 8, 8]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> [5, 5, 5, 8, 8]</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Forward fill copies the <strong>last known value forwards</strong>: 5 fills both gaps, and 8 fills the final gap. A copies backwards (that's bfill). C averages. B wrongly leaves the last gap even though a previous value (8) exists.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>What does:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.bfill()\n</code></pre>\n<p>do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Uses the following observation to fill a missing value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Uses the previous observation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Binarizes the column</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Removes outliers</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H29</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>s = [NaN, 4, NaN, NaN, 9, NaN]</code>. What does <code>s.bfill()</code> return?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> [4, 4, 9, 9, 9, NaN]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> [4, 4, 9, 9, 9, 9]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> [NaN, 4, 4, 4, 9, 9]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> [4, 4, 4, 4, 9, 9]</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Backward fill copies the <strong>next known value backwards</strong>: the first gap gets 4, the middle gaps get 9, and the last NaN has <strong>no later value</strong>, so it stays NaN. B invents a value for it. C is forward fill. D fills the middle gaps with the wrong value.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Missing-data scenario trap</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_impute\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Mean versus median imputation</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Change the outlier and see why the median is the safer fill value.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose only:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.2\\%$$</div>\n<p>of a very large dataset has missing values.</p>\n<p>Deleting those rows may be acceptable.</p>\n<p>But if:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$45\\%$$</div>\n<p>of rows contain missing information, blindly using:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">dropna()\n</code></pre>\n<p>could destroy a huge portion of your dataset.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly warns that indiscriminately deleting missing records can substantially reduce the dataset.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Duplicate questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>Which command counts duplicated rows?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">?\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>df.isna().sum()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>df.duplicated().sum()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>df.describe()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>df.groupby()</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H30</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A DataFrame has the rows (A, 10), (B, 20), (A, 10), (A, 10), (C, 30). What does <code>df.duplicated().sum()</code> return?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 5</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><code>duplicated()</code> marks a row as a duplicate only if an identical row has <strong>already appeared</strong>. The first (A, 10) is unmarked and the next two are marked, giving 2. A also counts the first occurrence. B and D are wrong.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>Which removes exact duplicates?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>df.drop_duplicates()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>df.dropna()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>df.ffill()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>df.to_numeric()</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H31</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Using the same rows, (A, 10), (B, 20), (A, 10), (A, 10), (C, 30), what does <code>df.drop_duplicates()</code> leave?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 2 rows: (B, 20) and (C, 30)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 3 rows: (A, 10), (B, 20) and (C, 30)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 5 rows, because nothing is removed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 4 rows</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><code>drop_duplicates()</code> keeps <strong>one copy</strong> of each distinct row (the first) and removes the repeats. A drops every copy of the repeated row. C and D miscount.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>Why should duplicates not always be automatically removed?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Pandas cannot remove them</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Some apparently identical records may represent genuine repeated events</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Duplicate rows are required for regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Duplicates improve model accuracy</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H32</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Two rows are identical: the same customer, product, price and date, with no time recorded. Should one be dropped?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Yes, always; identical rows are always errors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Yes, because pandas requires it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> No, never; duplicates are always genuine.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Not automatically: they could be two real purchases on the same day. Check a transaction ID or timestamp before deciding.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Duplicates can be <strong>errors or genuine repeated events</strong>. Without a field that tells them apart, dropping can delete real behaviour, and keeping can double-count. A and C overgeneralise. B is false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Duplicate leakage trap</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose the same observation appears twice:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Row A → Train set\nIdentical Row A → Test set\n</code></pre>\n<p>The model may effectively see the test observation during training.</p>\n<p>That can cause:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Data Leakage}}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture specifically notes duplicate records as a possible leakage source.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. Type conversion</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>A column contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;2026-10-03&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>but pandas stores it as <code>object</code>.</p>\n<p>Which function is appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>pd.to_datetime()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>pd.get_dummies()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>df.groupby()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>df.drop_duplicates()</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H33</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A date column contains \"2026-10-03\", \"03/10/2026\" and \"ERROR\". What should you watch for before relying on <code>pd.to_datetime()</code>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing; <code>to_datetime</code> always guesses correctly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Inconsistent formats (\"03/10/2026\" could mean 3 October or 10 March) and invalid entries (\"ERROR\") need deliberate handling, or dates will be wrong or the conversion will fail.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Dates should be one-hot encoded instead.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Convert the dates with <code>pd.to_numeric()</code> instead.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture's messy sensor example lists <strong>inconsistent date formats</strong> and \"ERROR\" values as things a model can't magically understand. Converting the type is correct, but only once the formats and invalid entries have been dealt with. A is overconfident. C and D are the wrong tools.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q34</span><div><p>A column contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;150.00&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>as text but should be numeric.</p>\n<p>Which function is appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>pd.to_numeric()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>pd.to_datetime()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>pd.qcut()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>pd.concat()</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H34</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A column contains \"$150.00\", \"1,200.50\" and \"99\". What is the correct approach?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Call <code>pd.to_numeric()</code> directly; it handles currency symbols.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Leave the column as text; models can read it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Remove the \"$\" and the thousands separators first, then convert with <code>pd.to_numeric()</code>.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> One-hot encode the values.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Symbols and commas make the values <strong>text</strong>, so they must be stripped before a numeric conversion: \"remove symbol → numeric\". A fails on \"$\" and \",\". B and D treat amounts as categories.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Filtering and selection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Know the conceptual distinction:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Column Selection}\n=\n\\text{Which variables}?$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Row Filtering}\n=\n\\text{Which observations}?$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q35</span><div><p>What does:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;price&quot;]\n</code></pre>\n<p>do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Selects the <code>price</code> column</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Selects the first row</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Removes the price column</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Calculates average price</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H35</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Classify each operation: (1) <code>df[\"price\"]</code>, (2) <code>df[df[\"price\"] &gt; 100]</code>, (3) <code>df.iloc[:, 1]</code>.</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> (1) row filtering, (2) column selection, (3) row filtering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> All three are row filtering.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> (1) column selection, (2) column selection, (3) row filtering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> (1) column selection, (2) row filtering, (3) column selection by position</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Column selection asks <strong>which variables</strong>: by name in (1), by position in (3) (<code>:</code> means all rows, <code>1</code> is the second column). Row filtering asks <strong>which observations</strong>: the Boolean condition in (2). A, B and C mix up the two ideas.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q36</span><div><p>What does:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[df[&quot;price&quot;] &gt; 100]\n</code></pre>\n<p>do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Keeps rows where price exceeds 100</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Replaces prices below 100</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sorts by price</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Selects only the column name</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H36</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>price = [80, 100, 150, 40, 120]</code>. How many rows does <code>df[df[\"price\"] &gt; 100]</code> keep?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Only 150 and 120 are <strong>strictly greater</strong> than 100. The value 100 itself fails <code>&gt; 100</code>. A wrongly includes 100 (that would be <code>&gt;= 100</code>). B and D miscount.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q37</span><div><p>What does <code>iloc</code> mainly select by?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Column labels only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Integer position</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Data type</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Missing-value count</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H37</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>The columns are, in order: date, price, product_id, quantity. What does <code>df.iloc[:, 1]</code> return?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The date column</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The first row</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The product_id column</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The price column</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><code>iloc</code> selects by <strong>integer position</strong>, starting from 0: position 0 is date, so position 1 is price. <code>:</code> keeps all rows. A forgets that positions start at 0. B confuses the row axis with the column axis. C counts from 1.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d18\">PART D - FEATURE ENGINEERING, ENCODING &amp; SCALING</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Feature engineering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q38</span><div><p>Which is feature engineering?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Delete the whole dataset</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Extract <code>month</code> from a timestamp</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Install pandas</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Save a CSV</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H38</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which step is <strong>feature engineering</strong> rather than data cleaning or type fixing?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Converting \"$150.00\" to 150.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Filling missing incomes with the median</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Removing exact duplicate rows</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Creating <code>days_since_last_purchase</code> from the purchase dates</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Feature engineering <strong>creates new, more useful information</strong> from existing data. A fixes a type. B imputes. C removes duplicates. All three are cleaning: they repair the data but don't add new features.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q39</span><div><p>What does:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;purchase_month&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.month\n</code></pre>\n<p>create?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A derived feature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A duplicate</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A database table</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H39</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>df[\"purchase_month\"] = df[\"date\"].dt.month</code> raises \"Can only use .dt accessor with datetimelike values\". What is the most likely cause?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The <code>date</code> column is stored as strings; convert it with <code>pd.to_datetime()</code> first.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The month doesn't exist.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>.dt.month</code> only works on numbers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The DataFrame has duplicates.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The <code>.dt</code> accessor needs a <strong>real datetime type</strong>. A date stored as text looks right to people but is just a string to pandas. That's why the lecture introduces <code>to_datetime()</code> before deriving features. B, C and D don't explain the error.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q40</span><div><p>What does:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;is_weekend&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.dayofweek &gt; 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>produce?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A Boolean weekend feature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A price category</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A text token</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A standardized score</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H40</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>pandas numbers the days from Monday = 0 to Sunday = 6. For a date that falls on a <strong>Friday</strong>, what does <code>df[\"date\"].dt.dayofweek &gt; 4</code> give, and which days give True?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> True; Friday, Saturday and Sunday give True</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> False; only Saturday (5) and Sunday (6) give True</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> True; only Friday gives True</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 4; it returns the day number</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Friday is 4, and <code>4 &gt; 4</code> is <strong>False</strong>. Only 5 (Saturday) and 6 (Sunday) pass, which makes this a Boolean weekend feature. A and C mishandle the strict inequality. D ignores the comparison.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Categorical encoding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l3_encoder\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Categorical encodings side by side</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Type your own categories and compare label, one-hot, dummy, effect and hashing encodings.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is a major exam topic.</p>\n<h5>Label encoding</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red   → 0\nBlue  → 1\nGreen → 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>Problem:</p>\n<p>The model may infer:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Green} &gt; \\text{Blue} &gt; \\text{Red}$$</div>\n<p>even though no such ranking exists.</p>\n<h5>One-hot encoding</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red   → [1,0,0]\nBlue  → [0,1,0]\nGreen → [0,0,1]\n</code></pre>\n<p>No artificial order.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly warns about the false magnitude problem.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Encoding MCQs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q41</span><div><p>Which method is generally better for <strong>nominal</strong> categories such as colour?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Arbitrary integer codes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> One-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Log transformation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Mean imputation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H41</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>When is integer coding of a categorical feature <strong>acceptable</strong> according to the lecture?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> When the categories are nominal, such as colours</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> When the categories have a genuine order, such as Low &lt; Medium &lt; High</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Never</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Whenever there are more than 10 categories</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Numerical coding makes sense when an <strong>ordering truly exists</strong>. For nominal categories (A) it invents a fake order. C ignores the lecture's \"when numerical coding makes sense\" section. D has nothing to do with order.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q42</span><div><p>Why may this be dangerous?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Malaysia = 1\nJapan = 2\nBrazil = 3\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Computers cannot process integers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The model may interpret the numbers as meaningful order/magnitude</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It always produces missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It creates a datetime</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H42</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>With Malaysia = 1, Japan = 2 and Brazil = 3 in a distance-based model, which false \"fact\" has the model been given?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> That the countries are missing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Nothing, because integer codes are neutral</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> That Japan is closer to Malaysia (distance 1) than Brazil is (distance 2), and that Brazil is \"3 times\" Malaysia</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> That all three countries are identical</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Integer codes bring <strong>distances and magnitudes</strong> with them, and a distance-based model will use them. \"Brazil &gt; Japan &gt; Malaysia\" is nonsense. B is the misconception. A and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q43</span><div><p>Which representation avoids implied order?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red = 1\nBlue = 2\nGreen = 3\n</code></pre></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red   = [1,0,0]\nBlue  = [0,1,0]\nGreen = [0,0,1]\n</code></pre></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H43</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Countries are one-hot encoded (Malaysia = [1, 0, 0], Japan = [0, 1, 0], Brazil = [0, 0, 1]). What is the Euclidean distance between <strong>any</strong> two different countries?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\sqrt{2}$ for every pair, so no country is \"closer\" to another</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1 for Malaysia and Japan, 2 for Malaysia and Brazil</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0, because they share zeros</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It depends on alphabetical order</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Any two different one-hot vectors differ in exactly two positions, so the distance is $\\sqrt{1^2 + 1^2} = \\sqrt{2}$, the <strong>same for every pair</strong>. That is precisely why one-hot avoids an implied order. B repeats the integer-coding problem.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q44</span><div><p>Which pandas function can perform one-hot encoding?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>pd.get_dummies()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>pd.to_datetime()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>df.dropna()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>df.info()</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H44</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A DataFrame has the columns <code>price</code> and <code>user_type</code> (values New, Premium, Regular). Which columns remain after <code>pd.get_dummies(df, columns=[\"user_type\"])</code>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> price, user_type, user_type_New, user_type_Premium, user_type_Regular</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> user_type_New, user_type_Premium, user_type_Regular</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> price, user_type_New, user_type_Premium</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> price, user_type_New, user_type_Premium, user_type_Regular</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><code>get_dummies</code> <strong>replaces</strong> the listed column with one indicator column per category and leaves the other columns alone. A keeps the original column. B drops <code>price</code>. C drops a category (that would need <code>drop_first=True</code>).</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Feature scaling</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_scaling\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Why feature scaling matters</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · How much of the distance between two customers comes from age versus income, before and after scaling.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age = 25\nIncome = 45000\n</code></pre>\n<p>In a distance calculation, income can numerically dominate simply because its units are much larger.</p>\n<p>The lecture specifically uses this example to motivate feature scaling, especially for algorithms such as KNN and SVM.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Scaling MCQs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q45</span><div><p>Why is feature scaling especially important for distance-based algorithms?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Large-scale variables can dominate distance calculations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It creates labels automatically</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It removes all outliers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It converts text into images</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H45</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Customer A is (age 25, income 50,000), B is (age 60, income 51,000) and C is (age 26, income 60,000). Using <strong>unscaled</strong> Euclidean distance, which customer is closer to A, and why does that matter?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> B (distance ≈ 1,000.6 vs ≈ 10,000.0 for C), even though B is 35 years older: income's large scale dominates the distance.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> C, because the ages are almost identical.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They are equally close.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Distance can't be computed with two features.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A-B: $\\sqrt{35^2 + 1000^2} \\approx 1{,}000.6$. A-C: $\\sqrt{1^2 + 10000^2} \\approx 10{,}000.0$. Income's larger numbers swamp age, which is the <strong>measuring-stick problem</strong> that scaling fixes. B is what you'd expect after scaling, not before.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q46</span><div><p>Which transformation usually maps values into approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[0,1]$$</div></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Standardization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Min-Max scaling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> One-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> TF-IDF</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H46</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A Min-Max scaler is fitted on training data with min = 20 and max = 40. A test value of 50 arrives. What is its scaled value, and is that a problem?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1; values above the maximum are capped automatically</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.5; going outside [0, 1] is fine, because the test set must use the training parameters</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.5; the scaler refits to the test data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An error; Min-Max can't handle new values</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$(50 - 20)/(40 - 20) = 30/20 = 1.5$. Min-Max maps <strong>training</strong> data to about [0, 1]. New data can fall outside that range, and that's honest: refitting on the test data (C) would be leakage. A and D are false for plain Min-Max scaling.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q47</span><div><p>Standardization generally transforms a feature toward:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Mean}=0$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{StandardDeviation}=1$$</div>\n<p>True or False?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> True</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> False</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture contrasts Min-Max scaling with Z-score standardization this way.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H47</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A feature has mean 50 and standard deviation 10. What is the standardized (Z-score) value of 35?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -1.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> -15</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.35</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$z = (x - \\mu)/\\sigma = (35 - 50)/10 = -1.5$: 35 is one and a half standard deviations <strong>below</strong> the mean. B drops the sign. C forgets to divide by σ. D divides by 100.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Simple scaling calculation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{min}=20$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{max}=40$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x=30$$</div>\n<p>Using Min-Max:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x'=\n\\frac{x-\\text{min}}{\\text{max}-\\text{min}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\n\\frac{30-20}{40-20}\n=\n\\frac{10}{20}\n=\n0.5$$</div>\n<p>So:</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q48</span><div><p>Min-Max scaled value of 30 is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H48</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>With training min = 20 and max = 40, what are the Min-Max scaled values of 25 and 15?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.25 and 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.25 and -0.25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.625 and 0.375</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.5 and -0.5</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$(25 - 20)/20 = 0.25$ and $(15 - 20)/20 = -0.25$. A value below the training minimum maps <strong>below 0</strong>. A clips it, which plain Min-Max doesn't do. C divides by $x$'s max instead of the range. D uses a range of 10.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d25\">PART E - TEXT, SUMMARISATION &amp; VISUALISATION</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. Basic text preprocessing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_text_pipeline\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Text preprocessing pipeline</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Type any sentence and watch each preprocessing step.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Lecture 2 only introduces the basics; don't turn this into Lecture 3-4 unnecessarily.</p>\n<p>Know:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Tokenization\nStopword removal\nStemming/Lemmatization\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture's example changes:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;The cats are running!&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>into roughly:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[&quot;cat&quot;, &quot;run&quot;]\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q49</span><div><p>Tokenization means:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scale numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Split text into units such as words</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Remove duplicates</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Create SQL tables</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H49</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>The lecture turns \"The cats are running!\" into roughly [\"cat\", \"run\"]. Which steps <strong>beyond</strong> tokenization were needed?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> None; tokenization alone gives [\"cat\", \"run\"].</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only lowercasing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scaling and one-hot encoding.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Removing punctuation, lowercasing, removing stopwords (\"the\", \"are\") and stemming or lemmatizing (\"cats\" → \"cat\", \"running\" → \"run\").</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Tokenization only <strong>splits</strong> the text into units: \"The\", \"cats\", \"are\", \"running\", \"!\". Getting to [\"cat\", \"run\"] also needs cleaning, stopword removal and normalisation. A overstates tokenization. B is incomplete. C belongs to numeric data.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q50</span><div><p>Stopwords are:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Always-important keywords</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Common terms that often carry little useful information</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Numeric outliers</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H50</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which tokens in \"the pump in the turbine is failing\" are stopword-like?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> pump, turbine</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> failing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> the, in, the, is</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> None; every word carries information</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Stopwords are <strong>very common function words</strong> that usually carry little information. \"pump\", \"turbine\" and \"failing\" carry the meaning. D ignores the idea of stopwords.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q51</span><div><p>Which best describes stemming/lemmatization?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Turn text into images</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reduce related word forms toward a root/canonical form</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Remove rows</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Detect duplicates</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H51</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>\"studies\", \"studying\" and \"studied\" → a stemmer gives \"studi\", while a lemmatizer gives \"study\". Which statement is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both group related word forms together; the stem may not be a real word, while the lemma is a canonical dictionary form.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only the lemmatizer groups the words together.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The stemmer is wrong, because it should give \"study\".</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both delete the words.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Both techniques <strong>reduce related forms</strong> to one shared form, so all three words count as the same feature. Stemming is heuristic, so it can produce non-words like \"studi\". That's allowed (C is wrong). B ignores that the stemmer also groups them. D is false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. <code>groupby()</code> - extremely likely practical MCQ</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture explains <code>groupby()</code> using:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Split → Apply → Combine\"><span>Split</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Apply</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Combine</span></div>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].mean()\n</code></pre>\n<p>means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Group observations by <code>user_type</code>, calculate average <code>price</code> in each group.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. <code>groupby()</code> questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q52</span><div><p>What does this calculate?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].mean()\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Overall price maximum</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mean price for each user type</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Number of missing prices</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Removes user types</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H52</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Prices: New = [100, 200, 300] and Premium = [500, 700]. Which statement is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>groupby(\"user_type\")[\"price\"].mean()</code> gives New 200 and Premium 600, while the overall mean price is 360 (not 400, the mean of the two group means).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The overall mean is 400, because it equals the average of the group means.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The grouped means are New 600 and Premium 1,200.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>groupby</code> returns one row per transaction.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The per-group means are $600/3 = 200$ and $1200/2 = 600$. The overall mean is $1800/5 = 360$. The average of the group means (400) ignores that the groups have <strong>different sizes</strong>. C gives sums, not means. D misdescribes the combine step.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q53</span><div><p>What is the conceptual process of <code>groupby()</code>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Train-Test-Evaluate</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Split-Apply-Combine</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Encode-Scale-Deploy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Crawl-Scrape-Store</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H53</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>For <code>df.groupby(\"region\")[\"sales\"].sum()</code>, which description of Split → Apply → Combine is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Split the columns; apply sorting; combine the files</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Split the rows by region; apply a sum to each region's sales; combine the results into one row per region</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Split train and test; apply the model; combine the predictions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Split the text into tokens; apply stemming; combine the results into Bag of Words</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><code>groupby</code> <strong>splits rows by the key</strong>, <strong>applies</strong> the aggregation within each group, and <strong>combines</strong> the results into a summary table. C and D describe other pipelines. A misreads every step.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q54</span><div><p>What does:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;quantity&quot;].sum()\n</code></pre>\n<p>calculate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Total quantity for each user type</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mean user type</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Number of columns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Maximum date</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H54</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Quantities: New = [2, 3, 5] and Premium = [1, 1]. Which result is <code>df.groupby(\"user_type\")[\"quantity\"].sum()</code>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> New 3.33, Premium 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> New 3, Premium 2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 12</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> New 10, Premium 2</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Sum per group: $2 + 3 + 5 = 10$ and $1 + 1 = 2$. A is <code>.mean()</code>. B is <code>.count()</code>. C is the overall total without grouping.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. Visualization</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_chart_picker\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Choosing a chart</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Pick the question; the right chart follows.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Know this table almost verbatim:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Chart</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Trend over time</td>\n<td>Line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare categories</td>\n<td>Bar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Numeric distribution</td>\n<td>Histogram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outliers/spread</td>\n<td>Box plot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relationship between two numbers</td>\n<td>Scatter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is exactly how the lecture presents chart selection.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. Visualization MCQs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q55</span><div><p>You want to show monthly revenue from January to December.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Histogram</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Line chart</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Box plot</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pie chart</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H55</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>You have monthly revenue for 2023, 2024 and 2025, and want to compare their <strong>seasonal patterns</strong>. Which chart is best?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A line chart with months on the x-axis and one line per year</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A pie chart of revenue per year</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A histogram of monthly revenue</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A box plot of each month</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Lines show <strong>how something changes over ordered time</strong>, and overlaying one line per year makes the seasonal shapes easy to compare. B loses the months. C loses the time order. D summarises spread rather than the pattern over time.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q56</span><div><p>You want to compare sales across:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">KL\nPenang\nIpoh\nJohor\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Bar chart</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Histogram</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scatter</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Line only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H56</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>You want to compare average sales per store across KL, Penang, Ipoh and Johor. Why is a <strong>bar</strong> chart better than a line chart?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Line charts can't show averages.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Bar charts are always more accurate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The cities are separate categories with no order; a line would suggest a continuous progression from one city to the next.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Line charts need at least 10 points.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Bar charts <strong>compare categories</strong>. Lines imply an ordered, continuous change, such as change over time, which doesn't exist between cities. A, B and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q57</span><div><p>You want to see the distribution of employee salaries.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Histogram</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Line</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scatter</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pie</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H57</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which pairing of question and chart is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> \"How are employee salaries distributed?\" → histogram</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> \"How are employee salaries distributed?\" → bar chart of the number of employees per department</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> \"Compare sales by city\" → histogram</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> \"How did revenue change month by month?\" → histogram</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A histogram bins <strong>one numerical variable</strong> to show its distribution. B compares categories, so it answers a different question. C (categories) needs a bar chart. D (change over time) needs a line chart.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q58</span><div><p>You want to identify potential outliers in salary.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Line</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Box plot</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Pie</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Map</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H58</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A box plot of salary shows several points far beyond the upper whisker. What do they indicate, and what should you do before imputing?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Missing values; drop them</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Potential outliers: check whether they are errors or genuine extreme values, and prefer the median if you impute</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Duplicates; drop them</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Perfect data; use mean imputation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Box plots summarise the centre and spread and <strong>flag extreme observations</strong>. Outliers distort the mean, so check them and lean towards median imputation. A and C misread the plot. D ignores what it shows.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q59</span><div><p>You want to investigate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{AdvertisingSpend}$$</div>\n<p>versus:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sales}$$</div></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scatter plot</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Histogram</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Pie chart</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Bar chart only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H59</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>You plot advertising spend against sales. Which chart is this, and what would <strong>no relationship</strong> look like?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A bar chart; all the bars the same height</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A histogram; a flat distribution</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A line chart; a straight horizontal line</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A scatter plot; a shapeless cloud of points with no upward or downward trend</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A scatter plot shows the <strong>relationship between two numerical variables</strong>, with one point per observation. A rising pattern suggests a positive association. A formless cloud suggests none. A, B and C are the wrong chart types for two numeric variables.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d31\">PART F - DATA LEAKAGE</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. The most important trap: data leakage</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l2_leakage\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Data leakage in scaling</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Fit the scaler on training data only, or on everything - and see what leaks.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Lecture 2 explicitly asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What is Data Leakage in preprocessing?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and defines it as using test-set information during preprocessing, such as recalculating scaling parameters with test data, which can falsely inflate performance.</p>\n<p>This is probably one of the highest-value concepts in the lecture.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. Leakage MCQs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q60</span><div><p>A scientist computes Min-Max scaling parameters using both training and test data before training.</p>\n<p>What is the main problem?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Overfitting only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Data leakage</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Underfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Clustering</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H60</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A Min-Max scaler is fitted on <strong>all</strong> the data. The test set contains an income of RM2,000,000; the largest income in the training set is RM300,000. What exactly leaked?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing, because scaling isn't learning.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The labels of the test set.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The scaler's maximum came from a test value, so every training income was scaled using information from the test set (and squashed towards 0).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The test rows were copied into the training set.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Min-Max <strong>learns</strong> min and max from the data it is fitted on. Fitting on everything lets a test value set the scale for the training features. That is preprocessing leakage. A is the misconception. B and D aren't what happened.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q61</span><div><p>What is the correct approach?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <pre><code class=\"language-python\">scaler.fit(X_train)\nX_train = scaler.transform(X_train)\nX_test = scaler.transform(X_test)\n</code></pre></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <pre><code class=\"language-python\">scaler.fit(X_train)\nscaler.fit(X_test)\n</code></pre></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H61</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which code is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>X_train = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)</code> then <code>X_test = scaler.fit_transform(X_test)</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>scaler.fit(X_test)</code> then transform both sets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>scaler.fit(pd.concat([X_train, X_test]))</code> then transform both sets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>X_train = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)</code> then <code>X_test = scaler.transform(X_test)</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>Fit on train only</strong>, then <strong>reuse</strong> those parameters on the test set. A refits on the test set, so the two sets end up on different scales. B learns from the test set. C learns from both, which is leakage.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q62</span><div><p>Why shouldn't you refit preprocessing independently on test/new data?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The model expects transformations consistent with training</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pandas prohibits it</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Test data cannot contain numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It automatically causes duplicates</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture's \"Crucial Rule\" is that deployment data must go through the <strong>same preprocessing</strong> learned during training.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H62</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>In deployment, a single new customer row is scaled by fitting a new Min-Max scaler <strong>on that one row</strong>. What happens?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It works perfectly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Min equals max, so the scaling breaks down (for example, division by zero), and the result doesn't match the training transformation in any case.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The model retrains itself.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The row becomes categorical.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>With one row, $\\max - \\min = 0$. Even with more rows, new data must go through <strong>the same preprocessing learned during training</strong>, which is the lecture's \"crucial rule\". A ignores both problems. C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d34\">PART G - CODING QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Coding Question 1 - inspection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">df.info()\n</code></pre>\n<p>What information is most likely produced?</p>\n<p>A. Model accuracy<br />\nB. Column types and non-null counts<br />\nC. A histogram<br />\nD. Encoded categories</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Coding Question 2 - missing values</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;price&quot;] = df[&quot;price&quot;].fillna(df[&quot;price&quot;].median())\n</code></pre>\n<p>What happens?</p>\n<p>A. Missing prices are removed<br />\nB. Missing prices are replaced with median price<br />\nC. Every price becomes the median<br />\nD. Duplicate prices are deleted</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. Coding Question 3 - duplicates</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">print(df.duplicated().sum())\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">18\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does this mean?</p>\n<p>A. Dataset has 18 columns<br />\nB. 18 rows are identified as duplicates under the default comparison<br />\nC. 18 values are missing<br />\nD. 18 features are categorical</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. Coding Question 4 - type conversion</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;date&quot;] = pd.to_datetime(df[&quot;date&quot;])\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>A. Make dates usable for date/time operations<br />\nB. One-hot encode dates<br />\nC. Calculate average date automatically<br />\nD. Remove duplicated timestamps</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. Coding Question 5 - categorical encoding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">pd.get_dummies(df, columns=[&quot;plan_type&quot;])\n</code></pre>\n<p>Suppose <code>plan_type</code> contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Basic\nPremium\nStudent\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does the code broadly create?</p>\n<p>A. Three binary indicator features<br />\nB. One continuous numerical feature<br />\nC. A datetime index<br />\nD. Missing values</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. Coding Question 6 - filtering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">high_value = df[df[&quot;price&quot;] &gt; 500]\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is <code>high_value</code>?</p>\n<p>A. Rows where price exceeds 500<br />\nB. Only the <code>price</code> column<br />\nC. Price mean<br />\nD. Prices scaled to 500</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. Coding Question 7 - engineered datetime feature</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;month&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.month\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is this?</p>\n<p>A. Data leakage automatically<br />\nB. Feature engineering<br />\nC. Duplicate removal<br />\nD. Standardization</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. Coding Question 8 - groupby</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">result = df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;quantity&quot;].sum()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>user_type</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">quantity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">7</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>What will approximately result?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">New      → 7\nPremium  → 10\n</code></pre>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$2+5=7$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$3+7=10$$</div></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d43\">PART H - HARDER SCENARIOS &amp; ANSWER KEYS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. Harder scenario questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q63</span><div><p>A dataset has:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age        25\nIncome     85000\nCountry    Malaysia\nSignup     2026-01-05\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which combination is most reasonable?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> TF-IDF Age, histogram Country</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Scale Age/Income where needed, encode Country, derive features from Signup</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Convert everything to strings</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Remove all non-numeric columns</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H63</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A dataset has <code>customer_id</code>, Age, Income, Country, Signup date and a Churn label, and a KNN model will be used. Which plan is best?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Use every column as it is, including <code>customer_id</code>.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Convert everything to strings and use TF-IDF.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scale every column, including Country and the ID.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Split first. Drop <code>customer_id</code>. Then, fitted on train only: scale Age and Income, one-hot encode Country, and derive features from Signup.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Each column type gets its own treatment, and everything is fitted on train only. The ID is an identifier, not information about the customer. KNN needs scaled numeric features. Country is nominal. Dates need extracted parts. A keeps the useless ID and unscaled values. B destroys the numeric meaning. C scales categories and identifiers.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q64</span><div><p>A model uses KNN with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age: 20-70\nIncome: 20,000-500,000\n</code></pre>\n<p>What should you be most concerned about?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Income may dominate the distance calculation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Age becomes text</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> KNN requires XML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> One-hot encoding income</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H64</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>For KNN, Age (20-70) and Income (20,000-500,000) are Min-Max scaled. After scaling, how much does a 10-year age gap contribute to the distance, compared with an RM48,000 income gap?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Age 0.2 and income 0.1: after scaling, this age gap counts for twice as much as that income gap.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Age 10 and income 48,000: income still dominates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both 0, because scaling removes the differences.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both exactly 1.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The age range is 50, so $10/50 = 0.2$. The income range is 480,000, so $48{,}000/480{,}000 = 0.1$. Scaling puts both features on comparable rulers. B describes the unscaled data. C and D misunderstand scaling.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q65</span><div><p>A categorical feature contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red\nBlue\nGreen\n</code></pre>\n<p>Someone maps:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red=1\nBlue=2\nGreen=3\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is the primary concern?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Data becomes too small</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> False ordinal/magnitude relationship</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Cannot calculate accuracy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Causes missing values</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H65</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>With Red = 1, Blue = 2 and Green = 3, a linear model can \"reason\" in ways that make no sense. Which example shows the problem?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Red and Green are both colours.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The model can't read the number 3.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The average of Red and Green is $(1 + 3)/2 = 2$, which is Blue, a meaningless relationship the encoding creates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The column now has missing values.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Integer labels allow <strong>arithmetic</strong> - averages, differences, \"Green = 3 x Red\" - that colours don't support. That's the false order and magnitude the lecture warns about. A is true but harmless. B and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q66</span><div><p>A time-series sensor reading is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">10:00  72\n10:01  NaN\n10:02  73\n</code></pre>\n<p>If the sensor value changes slowly and nearby state reasonably persists, which option may be sensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Forward fill</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> TF-IDF</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> SQL join</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H66</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Sensor readings: 10:00 = 72, 10:01 = NaN, 10:02 = 73, 10:03 = NaN, 10:04 = NaN, 10:05 = 90 (the machine was overheating). What does forward fill produce, and what is the risk?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 72, 72, 73, 73, 73, 90: the 10:03-10:04 gaps are filled with 73, which hides the climb towards 90</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 72, 73, 73, 90, 90, 90: no risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 72, 72.5, 73, 78.7, 84.3, 90: forward fill interpolates</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The NaN rows are deleted</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Forward fill copies the <strong>last known value</strong>, which suits slowly changing states. But when the value is changing quickly (overheating), it hides the trend. That's why the lecture calls missing-value handling a <strong>reasoning</strong> problem. B fills backwards. C describes interpolation, not forward fill. D describes <code>dropna()</code>.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q67</span><div><p>You want to investigate whether transaction amount increases as customer age increases.</p>\n<p>Which visualization?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scatter</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Histogram</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Bar</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pie</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H67</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Match each question to its chart: (1) Does transaction amount rise with customer age? (2) What does the distribution of transaction amounts look like? (3) How does total revenue compare across user types?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> (1) histogram, (2) scatter, (3) line</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> (1) bar, (2) line, (3) scatter</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> (1) scatter, (2) histogram, (3) bar</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A pie chart for all three</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Two numeric variables → <strong>scatter</strong>. One numeric variable's distribution → <strong>histogram</strong>. Comparing categories → <strong>bar</strong>. A and B swap the roles. D doesn't fit any of them well.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q68</span><div><p>You want to understand whether salary contains extreme observations before choosing mean imputation.</p>\n<p>Which visualization is especially useful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Box plot</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Line chart</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Pie chart</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Area chart</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H68</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Before imputing missing salaries, a box plot shows several extreme high values, which turn out to be genuine executive pay. Which imputation is most defensible?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Mean imputation, because the extreme values are genuine</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Median imputation, because genuine or not, the extreme values would pull the mean far above a typical salary</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Delete the executives so the mean works</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Fill with zeros</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Even genuine outliers <strong>distort the mean</strong>. The median stays representative of a typical employee. A misses the point. C throws away real data. D invents salaries of zero.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s45\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. Answer key for Q1-Q19</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">1</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">3</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">4</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">5</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">6</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">7</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">8</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">9</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">10</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">11</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">12</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">13</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">14</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">15</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">16</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">17</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">18</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">19</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s46\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">38. Answer key for Q20-Q44</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">20</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">21</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">22</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">23</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">24</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">25</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">26</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">27</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">28</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">29</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">30</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">31</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">32</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">33</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">34</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">35</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">36</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">37</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">38</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">39</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">40</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">41</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">42</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">43</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">44</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s47\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">39. Answer key for Q45-Q68</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">45</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">46</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">47</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">True</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">48</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">49</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">50</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">51</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">52</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">53</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">54</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">55</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">56</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">57</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">58</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">59</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">60</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">61</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">62</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">63</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">64</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">65</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">66</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">67</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">68</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l2p-d48\">PART I - FINAL REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s49\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">40. The code you should be able to recognise immediately</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You probably don't need to write huge programs. For an MCQ with some coding, I'd make sure these are instantly familiar:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Inspect\ndf.info()\ndf.describe()\n\n# Missing values\ndf.isna().sum()\ndf.dropna()\ndf[&quot;price&quot;].fillna(df[&quot;price&quot;].median())\ndf.ffill()\ndf.bfill()\n\n# Duplicates\ndf.duplicated().sum()\ndf.drop_duplicates()\n\n# Types\ndf[&quot;date&quot;] = pd.to_datetime(df[&quot;date&quot;])\ndf[&quot;price&quot;] = pd.to_numeric(df[&quot;price&quot;])\n\n# Filtering\ndf[df[&quot;price&quot;] &gt; 100]\n\n# Feature engineering\ndf[&quot;month&quot;] = df[&quot;date&quot;].dt.month\n\n# One-hot encoding\npd.get_dummies(df, columns=[&quot;user_type&quot;])\n\n# Summarization\ndf.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].mean()\n</code></pre>\n<p>You should understand <strong>what each line does</strong>, rather than waste time memorising punctuation.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2p-s50\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">41. The exam traps I'd prioritize</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The three I would drill hardest are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{CRISP}\\text{-}\\text{DM vs ML Pipeline}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Label Encoding vs OneHot}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Preprocessing Consistency}\\ / \\text{Data Leakage}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Then make sure you're comfortable with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{dropna vs fillna vs ffill/bfill}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{info vs describe}$$</div>\n<p>plus chart selection.</p>\n<p>The lecture's own final assessment emphasizes essentially these same things: preprocessing, missing-value handling, one-hot encoding, scaling, <code>groupby()</code>, chart selection and data leakage.</p>\n<p>A very good final mental model for Lecture 2 is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Raw Data → Inspect → Clean → Transform → Encode/Scale → Engineer → Model → Evaluate\"><span>Raw Data</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Inspect</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Clean</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transform</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Encode/Scale</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Engineer</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Model</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluate</span></div>\n<p>with the golden rule:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Fit preprocessing on training data},\\ \\text{then reuse it on test/new data}.\n}$$</div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l2-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Bloom challenge - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry, coding, hackathon, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the lecture&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"2\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Lecture 2 Bloom challenge</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"2\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which sequence is the correct CRISP-DM phase order?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Data Understanding → Business Understanding → Data Preparation → Modeling → Evaluation → Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Business Understanding → Data Preparation → Data Understanding → Modeling → Evaluation → Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Business Understanding → Data Understanding → Data Preparation → Modeling → Evaluation → Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Business Understanding → Data Understanding → Modeling → Data Preparation → Evaluation → Deployment</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Business → Data Understanding → Data Preparation → Modeling → Evaluation → Deployment.</strong> Each distractor swaps one adjacent pair. A starts with data before the problem is defined; B prepares data before understanding it; D models before preparing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which <strong>single</strong> pandas call shows each column's data type <strong>and</strong> its non-null count?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>df.info()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>df.describe()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>df.isna().sum()</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>df.dtypes</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><code>info()</code> shows the row count, columns, dtypes and non-null counts together. <code>describe()</code> gives statistics (mean, std, min, max) but no dtypes. <code>isna().sum()</code> gives missing counts only. <code>dtypes</code> gives types only.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>How does the lecture define data leakage <strong>in preprocessing</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Losing rows when <code>dropna()</code> is applied.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Using information from the test set - such as recalculating scaling parameters on it - which falsely inflates accuracy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Saving the trained model to a public repository.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Training on more columns than the model needs.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>That is the lecture's own definition, from its final assessment slide. A is data loss, not leakage. C is a security issue. D concerns feature selection.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why must a deployed pipeline <strong>reuse</strong> the min/max learned on training data instead of recomputing them for each incoming customer?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because recomputing is slower.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because the deployment server cannot import scikit-learn.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because a single customer has no min or max, so the result would be 0.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because the model learned on features defined by those parameters; recomputing changes what a value means (RM10,000 would no longer map to ≈ 0.444).</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Preprocessing <strong>defines the representation</strong> the model was trained on. If the ruler changes at deployment, identical raw inputs become different features and predictions break silently. A and B are incidental. C is half-true for a single row, but the principle holds for whole batches too: recomputing on new data shifts the meaning of every feature.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>For a <strong>linear</strong> model, why is one-hot encoding usually better than label encoding for <code>Country = Malaysia / Japan / Brazil</code>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Label encoding makes the model treat countries as ordered, evenly spaced quantities (Brazil = 2 x Japan), relationships that don't exist.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> One-hot encoding uses fewer columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Label encoding cannot be stored in pandas.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> One-hot encoding sorts the countries alphabetically.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A linear model multiplies each feature by a weight, so codes 0/1/2 impose an <strong>order and equal spacing</strong>. One-hot gives each country its own indicator with no implied magnitude. B is backwards - one-hot uses <em>more</em> columns. C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does feature scaling change k-NN's predictions but <strong>not</strong> the split thresholds chosen by a decision tree?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Trees ignore numeric features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> k-NN combines features into one distance, so large-unit features dominate; a tree splits on one feature at a time by threshold, and min-max or z-scaling keeps each feature's ordering.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scaling changes the labels for k-NN only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Decision trees always scale data internally.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture's rule is to ask whether the algorithm is <strong>sensitive to feature magnitude</strong>. Distances mix units (income dwarfs age). A tree compares one feature against a threshold, and monotonic rescaling leaves the order of values - and therefore the same partitions - unchanged. A, C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Min-max scaling was fitted on training incomes from RM2,000 to RM20,000. A new customer earns <strong>RM26,000</strong>. With the training parameters reused, what is the scaled value?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1.000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.333</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.444</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.000</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$(26{,}000 - 2{,}000) / (20{,}000 - 2{,}000) = 24{,}000 / 18{,}000 \\approx 1.333$. Values outside the training range map <strong>outside 0-1</strong>, and that is correct and honest. A assumes clipping, which <code>MinMaxScaler</code> doesn't do by default. C is RM10,000's value. D is what you'd get by \"refitting\" on a single row.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A feature has values <code>2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9</code> (mean 5, population standard deviation 2). What is the z-score of 9?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.5</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$z = (9 - 5)/2 = 2.0$: two standard deviations above the mean. B forgets to divide by σ. A and D use the wrong spread. <code>StandardScaler</code> uses this population standard deviation (ddof = 0).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Given:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>user_type</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">700</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Returning</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">150</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Returning</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">250</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>What is the <code>mean</code> value for <strong>Returning</strong> in <code>df.groupby(\"user_type\")[\"price\"].agg([\"mean\", \"max\"])</code>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 250</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 150</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 400</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 200</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Split → Returning: {150, 250}; apply mean → <strong>200</strong>; combine into one row per group. A is that group's max. B is its min. C is the sum.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p><code>price</code> has 3% missing values, the rows are independent purchases (not a time series), and the distribution has a few extreme prices. Which strategy fits best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Forward fill.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Median imputation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Mean imputation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Drop every column that contains a missing value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Independent rows → no meaningful \"previous\" value, so A is unjustified. Extreme values pull the mean, so C is distorted. Dropping the whole <code>price</code> column (D) throws away a key feature to fix 3% of its cells. The <strong>median</strong> keeps the rows and is robust to outliers - the lecture's exact guidance.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A notebook does this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">X_scaled = MinMaxScaler().fit_transform(X)\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X_scaled, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is the problem?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> MinMaxScaler cannot be used before splitting for syntax reasons.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The scaler learned min/max from rows that end up in the test set, so test information leaked into preprocessing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scaling should only be applied to the target y.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> There is no problem, because min-max scaling doesn't change the order of values.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The fit saw <strong>all</strong> rows, so the test set influenced the parameters - the lecture's definition of preprocessing leakage. The fix is to split first, fit on train, then transform both. D is the trap: keeping each column's order doesn't stop the test data from shaping the transform.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Two rows look identical - same customer, item, price and timestamp - except <code>transaction_id</code> is 401 vs 402. <code>df.duplicated().sum()</code> returns <strong>0</strong>. Why, and what should you check?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>duplicated()</code> is broken on timestamps; convert them to strings.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> pandas treats the first row as unique, so the count is always 0.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>duplicated()</code> compares all columns by default, and the IDs differ; check <code>df.duplicated(subset=[...])</code> without the ID, then use domain logic to decide whether it's double-logging or two real purchases.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The rows are definitely two real purchases, so no check is needed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>By default every column must match, so a unique ID hides near-duplicates. Using <code>subset=</code> finds them. Then the lecture's rule applies: <strong>don't delete automatically</strong> - decide whether these are legitimate repeat events. A and B are false. D skips the reasoning step.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A manager asks: \"Across our 50 stores, does advertising spend relate to sales, and are there unusual stores?\" Which chart answers this best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A scatter plot of ad spend vs sales, one point per store.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A line chart of sales by store ID.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A bar chart of the 50 stores' sales.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A histogram of sales.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A relationship between <strong>two numeric variables</strong> calls for a scatter plot, and outlying stores stand out as points far from the trend. B implies an order (time) that store IDs don't have. C compares one variable across categories. D shows one variable's distribution, so it can't show a relationship.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>In a 1,000-row dataset, 450 rows are missing <code>income</code>, and you suspect high earners chose not to report it. Which choice is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>dropna()</code>, because missing data can't be trusted.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fill with the mean, because it preserves the average.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Forward fill, because it uses real observed values.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Keep the rows, impute with a robust statistic, and record that income was missing, because the missingness itself may carry information.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Dropping loses <strong>45%</strong> of the data (A). If high earners hide income, the mean of the <em>observed</em> values underestimates them, so B adds bias. C is meaningless for unordered rows. The lecture frames missing-value handling as a <strong>reasoning problem</strong>: keep the data, use a robust fill, and don't hide the fact that it was missing.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>An API returns JSON customers, each with a nested list of orders. You must predict <strong>customer churn</strong>. Which flattening is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One row per order, repeating the customer's fields on each row.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> One row per customer, with aggregated order features (order count, total spend, days since last order).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Drop the orders, because nested data can't be used in ML.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> One row per order, with churn copied to each order row.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The prediction unit is the <strong>customer</strong>, so each row should be one customer. A and D multiply each customer by their order count. That over-weights heavy buyers, and the same customer's rows can land in both train and test, a leak. C throws away the most predictive behaviour. Aggregation turns nested data into the rows = observations, columns = features shape.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which ordering builds a <strong>deployment-safe</strong> preprocessing workflow?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Impute → scale → encode on all data → split → train → evaluate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Split → fit imputer, encoder and scaler on train → transform train and test → train → evaluate → save the fitted preprocessors with the model → apply them unchanged to new data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Split → fit preprocessors separately on train and on test → train → evaluate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Train on raw data → evaluate → add preprocessing only in production.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>This combines the lecture's two big rules: <strong>split first</strong> (no leakage) and <strong>reuse the training parameters</strong> (consistency) - including at deployment, which is why the fitted preprocessors are saved. A leaks. C re-learns parameters on test data. D creates a mismatch between training and production inputs.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You must predict hourly restaurant sales from a raw <code>timestamp</code>. Which engineered feature set is most useful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The raw timestamp string only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>hour</code>, <code>day_of_week</code>, <code>is_weekend</code> and a public-holiday flag derived from the timestamp.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A label-encoded version of the full timestamp string.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The timestamp's character length.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Sales follow <strong>daily and weekly cycles and holidays</strong>. Derived parts expose those patterns directly - the lecture's weekend example, extended. A and C give a mostly unique value per row with no learnable pattern. D is meaningless.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A new dataset arrives. Which plan should come <strong>before</strong> any modelling?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fit three models and keep whichever scores best.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Drop every row with any missing value, then model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scale every column, including the IDs, then inspect.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>info()</code> for types and nulls, <code>describe()</code> for ranges and outliers, <code>duplicated()</code> checks, <code>value_counts()</code> on categories, and a histogram or box plot of key numeric columns.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>\"Inspect before touching.\" This covers the lecture's data-quality checklist: missing values, wrong types, duplicates and extreme outliers, plus visualisation. A models blind. B destroys data before understanding it. C transforms meaningless columns.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p><code>income</code> is missing mostly for high earners who refuse to disclose it. Which missing-data mechanism is this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> MCAR - missing completely at random.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> MAR - missing at random given the other observed variables.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Not a missing-data problem, because the value is recorded as NaN.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> MNAR - missing not at random: the probability of missingness depends on the unobserved income itself.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chance of being missing depends on the <strong>value that is missing</strong> (high income → refuses). That is MNAR, the hardest case: no simple imputation is unbiased, which is why the lecture warns that mean filling can introduce bias. MCAR would be random loss. MAR would depend only on observed fields, such as job title.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p><code>RobustScaler</code> computes $(x - \\text{median}) / \\text{IQR}$. For the values <code>10, 12, 13, 15, 1000</code> (median 13, Q1 = 12, Q3 = 15), what is the scaled value of 15?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.667</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.002</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 329.0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$(15 - 13)/(15 - 12) = 2/3 \\approx 0.667$. D is the outlier 1000's value: it stays extreme, but it no longer squashes everyone else. B mimics min-max, where 1000 stretches the range and every normal value collapses near 0. That's why robust scaling is preferred for outlier-heavy features, building on the lecture's mean-vs-median logic.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>Using the same six-row table as the <code>groupby</code> question (Premium prices 500 and 700), what is <code>dev</code> for the Premium row with price <strong>700</strong>?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;dev&quot;] = df[&quot;price&quot;] - df.groupby(&quot;user_type&quot;)[&quot;price&quot;].transform(&quot;mean&quot;)\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 100</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> NaN</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 600</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><code>transform(\"mean\")</code> broadcasts each group's mean back to <strong>every original row</strong> (same length as <code>df</code>). Premium's mean is 600, so 700 - 600 = <strong>100</strong>. <code>agg</code> would return one row per group and couldn't be subtracted row-wise; that's the agg-vs-transform distinction. D is the group mean itself.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>What is wrong with this evaluation, and what fixes it?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">X_scaled = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)\nscores = cross_val_score(KNeighborsClassifier(), X_scaled, y, cv=5)\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing - cross-validation already separates the folds.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Use <code>cv=10</code>; five folds are too few for k-NN.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The scaler saw every validation fold before CV began; wrap both steps in <code>make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), KNeighborsClassifier())</code> and cross-validate the pipeline.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> k-NN must not be scaled.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Each CV fold acts as a mini test set, but the scaler's mean and std were computed with those rows included - the lecture's leakage rule applied <strong>inside</strong> cross-validation. A <code>Pipeline</code> re-fits the scaler on each training fold only. A is the common misconception. B keeps the leak. D contradicts the lecture: k-NN is distance-based and needs scaling.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A churn model reaches 0.99 AUC in validation but fails in production. One feature is <code>days_since_cancellation_request</code>. What happened?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Target leakage: the feature only exists <strong>after</strong> a customer has started to churn, so it isn't available at prediction time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Underfitting: the model needs more trees.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Covariate shift in production.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The validation split was too small.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A feature that is only recorded <em>because</em> the outcome is happening encodes the label itself, which makes the model look perfect offline. At decision time (before the cancellation request) the value doesn't exist. It is leakage in the same spirit as the lecture's warning: information the model wouldn't really have. B, C and D don't explain a near-perfect score.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Kidney-stone data: Treatment A succeeds in 93% of small-stone cases and 73% of large-stone cases. Treatment B succeeds in 87% (small) and 69% (large). Yet <strong>overall</strong>, A has 78% and B 83%. What explains this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A calculation error, because aggregates must preserve subgroup order.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Simpson's paradox: A was given mostly to harder large-stone cases, so stone size confounds the aggregate comparison.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Treatment B is better; subgroup results should be ignored.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Random noise, which disappears with more data.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A is better within <strong>each</strong> subgroup but worse overall, because the groups have very different mixes of easy and hard cases. Summaries (the lecture's <code>groupby</code> step) can reverse a conclusion when a confounder is ignored. Choose the grouping from the causal question being asked, not by default. A is false - aggregation can reverse order. C draws the wrong causal conclusion. D: the effect is structural, so more data won't remove it.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Offline AUC is 0.90, online AUC is 0.70. Logs show the training pipeline used income in RM, while the production service sends income in thousands of RM. What is this, and what fix prevents it?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Concept drift; retrain weekly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Overfitting; add regularisation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Training-serving skew; ship the <em>same</em> fitted preprocessing pipeline with the model so both paths share one transformation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Label noise; relabel the data.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The same customer gets different feature values offline and online - the industrial version of the lecture's <strong>preprocessing consistency</strong> rule. The robust fix is a single artefact (model plus fitted transformers) used everywhere. A and B treat statistical problems; this is a pipeline-mismatch bug.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p>You are forecasting tomorrow's demand from a daily time series. Which preprocessing step leaks <strong>future</strong> information?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Forward fill (<code>ffill</code>) of missing days.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Lag features such as yesterday's demand.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Backward fill (<code>bfill</code>) of missing days.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A rolling mean of the previous 7 days, shifted by one day.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><code>bfill</code> copies the <strong>next</strong> known value backwards, so a past row uses information from its future. In a forecasting set-up, that is leakage. <code>ffill</code> (A), lags (B) and shifted rolling windows (D) only use past values. The lecture links ffill/bfill to ordered data; the extension is checking the <em>direction</em> of the fill.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p>A medical-imaging hackathon has 20,000 X-rays from 4,000 patients (about 5 images each). Random 5-fold CV gives 0.96, while the hidden test set (new patients) gives 0.81. Which validation is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Stratified K-fold on the label.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> More folds with random splitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>GroupKFold</code> with <code>patient_id</code> as the group, so no patient appears in both train and validation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Validate on the training set to avoid wasting data.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Images of the same patient are near-duplicates. Random folds put them on both sides, which is the lecture's <strong>duplicate-leakage</strong> trap at scale. Grouping by patient simulates \"new patients\". A and B keep the leak. D measures memorisation.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Before imputing <code>income</code>, you add a binary column <code>income_missing</code>. When is this most likely to help?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Never - it duplicates the imputed value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> When missingness is informative (MAR/MNAR, e.g. high earners refusing), because the model can learn from the fact that a value was missing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only when data is MCAR.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only for k-NN models.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>If missingness correlates with the target, the indicator carries signal that imputation alone hides. Under MCAR, being missing is pure chance, so the indicator adds little (C is backwards). A ignores that the imputed value and the indicator encode different facts. D is false - tree and linear models use indicators well.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Two models score 96% and 97% accuracy. Which visualisation would most mislead a decision-maker?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A bar chart whose y-axis starts at 0.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A bar chart whose y-axis starts at 95%, making one bar look twice as tall as the other.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A dot plot with confidence intervals.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A table listing both scores and their test-set sizes.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Truncating the axis turns a 1-point difference into an apparent 2x difference. The lecture says chart choice must follow the <strong>question</strong>; the extension is that chart <em>design</em> can distort the answer. C and D add uncertainty and context, which is more honest.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Six months after launch, a retrained model gives different results, and nobody can reproduce the original. Which practice set best prevents this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Keep only the final model file.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Retrain on whatever data is in the database today.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Record the experiment in a slide deck.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Version the raw data snapshot, code, fitted preprocessing parameters, model and random seeds together, so any model can be rebuilt exactly.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Reproducibility needs <strong>every input</strong> that shaped the model, including the fitted preprocessing (the lecture's \"same parameters\" rule extended across time). A loses the preprocessing and the data. B silently changes the data. C isn't executable.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
